  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Choosing the right location for your home in Texas matters more than almost any floor-plan decision, because you can remodel a kitchen but you cannot move a lot. The best spot balances your lifestyle, your budget (including property taxes), and practical risks like flooding and soil. Use the checklist below before you put money down on land.</p>

<h2>Start with how you want to live</h2>
<p>Texas gives you every option: dense neighborhoods in Houston, Dallas, Austin and San Antonio, fast-growing suburbs, small towns, and acreage out in the country. Be honest about what your days look like.</p>
<ul>
<li>How far are you willing to drive to work, school, groceries and a hospital?</li>
<li>Do you want walkable restaurants, or room for a shop, garden or animals?</li>
<li>Do you need reliable high-speed internet for remote work?</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are torn between city and country, our post on <a href="/the-pros-and-cons-of-building-in-urban-vs-rural-texas/">building in urban vs. rural Texas</a> breaks down the trade-offs in detail.</p>

<h2>Climate and natural hazards</h2>
<p>Texas climates range from humid Gulf Coast to dry West Texas, and each region brings its own building concerns:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Gulf Coast</strong>: hurricanes, heavy rain, humidity and flood risk. Windstorm requirements may apply near the coast; check with the Texas Department of Insurance and your local building department.</li>
<li><strong>Central Texas</strong>: flash flooding in Hill Country creeks, rocky or shifting soils, and wildfire risk in some areas.</li>
<li><strong>North Texas</strong>: hail, severe storms and expansive clay soils that affect foundation design.</li>
<li><strong>West Texas</strong>: intense sun, wind and water availability.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Check flood risk carefully</h3>
<p>Look up any lot on the <a href="https://msc.fema.gov/portal/home">FEMA Flood Map Service Center</a>, then call the city or county floodplain administrator. Local drainage rules and updated maps can be stricter than older FEMA maps, and homes outside mapped zones can still flood. Ask neighbors, and visit after a big rain if you can.</p>

<h2>Soil, utilities and the lot itself</h2>
<p>Two lots a mile apart can cost very differently to build on. Before you buy, find out:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Soil conditions.</strong> Much of Texas has expansive clay that swells and shrinks. A geotechnical report helps your engineer design the right foundation.</li>
<li><strong>Utilities.</strong> Is there city water and sewer, or will you need a well and septic system? How far away is electric service?</li>
<li><strong>Access and easements.</strong> Is there legal road access? Are there pipeline, utility or drainage easements limiting where you can build?</li>
<li><strong>Deed restrictions and HOA rules.</strong> Minimum square footage, materials, fencing and outbuildings are often restricted.</li>
<li><strong>Zoning and setbacks.</strong> These decide what and where you can build on the parcel.</li>
</ul>
<p>Rules differ by city and county. Our guide to <a href="/navigating-texas-building-codes-what-you-need-to-know/">navigating Texas building codes</a> explains who regulates what.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Walk the lot yourself with a notebook, a measuring tool and your phone camera. Mark low spots where water sits, note which direction the back of the house would face, and measure to the nearest power pole or utility box. Those notes are gold when you talk to builders and engineers.</p></div>
<p>A [[amz:laser_measure|laser distance measure]] makes quick work of setbacks and distances, and a [[amz:graph_paper_notebook|graph paper notebook]] is handy for rough sketches of the site.</p>
<!--products:laser_measure,graph_paper_notebook,headlamp|Handy gear for your first lot walk-->

<h2>Taxes and total cost of living</h2>
<p>Texas has no state income tax, but property taxes are where local costs show up. Your total rate combines county, city, school district and special districts such as Municipal Utility Districts (MUDs), which are common in newer suburban subdivisions. Look up the address on the county appraisal district website and add up every taxing entity. Also compare insurance quotes, since wind, hail and flood exposure can move premiums a lot.</p>

<h2>Schools, commute, services and safety</h2>
<p>Even if you do not have kids, school district quality tends to affect resale value. Review the Texas Education Agency accountability ratings, then visit schools if they matter to you. Drive your commute at rush hour, not on a Sunday afternoon. Check the distance to grocery stores, healthcare, and emergency services. In rural areas, find out which fire department covers the property, since that can affect insurance.</p>

<h3>Safety and the neighborhood's direction</h3>
<p>Look at local crime data through city or county law enforcement, and walk the area at different times of day. Then look forward: check the city's planning and zoning notices and road projects. A new highway or commercial development can boost convenience and value, or bring noise and traffic right behind your back fence.</p>

<h2>Resale value and your exit plan</h2>
<p>Even a forever home may not be forever. Jobs move, families grow and parents age. Before you commit to a lot, ask yourself who would buy this house from you in ten years and what they would want. A few location traits tend to hold value across most Texas markets:</p>
<ul>
<li>Reasonable access to major job centers</li>
<li>A well-regarded school district</li>
<li>Low flood risk and good drainage</li>
<li>Reliable utilities and internet</li>
<li>Few nearby nuisances such as rail lines, heavy industry or a planned highway expansion</li>
</ul>
<p>A local real estate agent can pull recent sales so you can see how comparable homes have held their value. You do not need to build for the market, but you should avoid building the most expensive house in a neighborhood where buyers will not pay for it.</p>

<h2>Next steps</h2>
<p>Once you have a shortlist of areas, price the build for each one with our <a href="/budgeting-for-your-texas-home-build-tips-for-staying-on-track/">Texas home build budgeting guide</a>, and read <a href="/how-much-does-it-cost-to-build-a-house-in-texas/">how much it costs to build a house in Texas</a> to factor in site work. The <a href="/free-training/">free training</a> covers how to evaluate land as part of planning your build.</p>
